# REQUIRES: clang, x86-target
#
# THE CAPTURE-POINT BOUNDARY, as an executable assertion.
#
# This is the missing middle of the clangover case. p4-risk/clangover-x86-codegen.test
# already asserts what the x86 ASSEMBLY contains (btl / jae). This test asserts
# what the PRE-ISEL LLVM IR contains -- the artifact a frozen-LLVM relational
# verifier actually reads -- and the answer is: no secret-dependent branch at all.
#
# The three levels of the same translation unit:
#
#   .c   branchless by construction   (mask = -bit; out = mask & 1665)
#   .ll  branchless `select i1`       <-- THIS TEST
#   .s   btl + jae                    (p4-risk/clangover-x86-codegen.test)
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. A verifier that freezes pre-ISel and proves
# a relational property over the frozen module will find secret-INDEPENDENT
# control flow here, and will be correct to do so. The leak is introduced by
# instruction selection, below the capture point. clangover is therefore a P4
# phenomenon, and a claim that an LLVM-level relational proof "catches clangover"
# is false.
#
# Keeping that as a test rather than as prose means it cannot quietly rot: if a
# future LLVM starts emitting the branch in the middle end instead, the branch
# count below changes and the classification must be revisited.
#
# These checks make no ModelStatus claim.

# --- vulnerable form: the secret-dependent op is a select, not a branch -----
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -Os -fno-vectorize -fno-slp-vectorize -S -emit-llvm %harness/c/clangover_poly_frommsg_vulnerable.c -o %t.bad.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR-BAD --input-file=%t.bad.ll

# The message bit selects between 0 and the coefficient, branchlessly.
# IR-BAD-DAG: select i1
# The only two branches are the loop back-edges, on public trip counts.
# IR-BAD-DAG: icmp eq i64 {{.*}}, 32
# IR-BAD-DAG: icmp eq i64 {{.*}}, 8

# Exactly two `br i1` exist, which together with the two DAG matches above
# accounts for every branch in the function. None tests the message byte.
# RUN: grep -c "br i1" %t.bad.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=BAD-BRCOUNT
# BAD-BRCOUNT: 2

# --- fixed form: branchless at IR level too, via an outlined cmov -----------
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -Os -fno-vectorize -fno-slp-vectorize -S -emit-llvm %harness/c/clangover_poly_frommsg_fixed.c -o %t.fixed.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR-FIXED --input-file=%t.fixed.ll
# IR-FIXED-DAG: @clangover_ct_cmov
# IR-FIXED-NOT: select i1

# RUN: grep -c "br i1" %t.fixed.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FIXED-BRCOUNT
# FIXED-BRCOUNT: 2

# --- the differential that names the boundary -------------------------------
# Same translation unit, same flags, one step further down the pipeline: the
# branch appears. This pair of facts is what locates the defect below the freeze
# point rather than inside the frozen module.
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -Os -fno-vectorize -fno-slp-vectorize -S %harness/c/clangover_poly_frommsg_vulnerable.c -o %t.bad.s
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ASM-BAD --input-file=%t.bad.s
# ASM-BAD-LABEL: clangover_poly_frommsg_vulnerable:
# ASM-BAD: btl
# ASM-BAD-NEXT: jae

# CONSEQUENCE FOR FIXTURE CLASSIFICATION.
#
# mlir/clangover_poly_frommsg.lowered_bad.mlir models this case as an
# `llvm.cond_br` at LLVM-dialect level. Its own header is scrupulous about this
# -- "classification: modeled-from-verified-assembly", "artifact status:
# hand-written target model derived from verified assembly" -- and that labelling
# should be read as marking a P4 case rendered in LLVM-dialect syntax for
# convenience, NOT as a claim that the frozen module contains a branch. This test
# is the evidence for that reading.
#
# INCIDENTAL OBSERVATION, not asserted here. The fixed form's call is emitted as
# `tail call`. Profile section 8.4 rejects `tail`, `musttail`, `notail`, `invoke`
# and `callbr` in v1, so the fixed artifact would need the marker removed or the
# call re-emitted before it could enter the normal form. That is a normal-form
# question rather than a confidentiality one.
